y no desire to do  that any anymore you know i  did when i started but not now but i  really wish somebody had  made it known to me that like you don't  necessarily have to be  quentin tarantino to make a living  making movies  it's interesting how  people's desires for success change and  maybe you get tastes of it here and you  realize you know what this is a lot of  stress over here i kind of like this  realm where i'm doing my own thing i'm  still accountable but  i don't have to be you know  there's a price to pay i think for  certain levels of a career yeah yeah  totally and one thing that's always been  a  not a problem but a you know a source of  anxiety for me especially when i have  outside investors like  you know i feel you know you know a  moral and you know and of course a you  know legal responsibility to return  the money and when you're making movies  for fifty thousand dollars or sixty  thousand dollars or twenty thousand  dollars  most of the people that are investing in  those movies are not super rich people  you know because like real like you know  rich you know experienced investors  they're not going to invest in a 60 000  movie you know and not because it can't  be profitable but because  you know there's a ceiling to how  profitable it can be  you know like yes sky is the limit but  you know it's outliers you know for the  most part if you do a hundred movies at  50 000 the most one of those is going to  make in a year  in general terms is you know like maybe  a couple hundred thousand and that's  like at the tippity top so for a you  know a regular investor somebody that  you know invests millions of dollars or  you know they have big they're that's  just not an exciting investment they're  like what i'm going to put 60 000 and  you know two years later i'm gonna get  150 back or you know maybe 200 it's just  not  you know so it's  so the people that are investing in  these movies are usually  you know people like myself you know  that yo